  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Archive",
    "overview": "Kothai is a private room for things you kept. The interface uses only black, white and opacity, so the colour on screen comes from what you saved: a video thumbnail, a photo, an article's lead image. Chrome recedes so the archive can be seen. The quiet is structural, not timid: every value on screen comes from a closed scale, so nothing looks improvised.\n\nDensity is that of a working tool. Body text is 14px, controls are 32px tall (44px on touch), and a page opens with one title row and one toolbar row. Hierarchy comes from ink opacity, size and weight, never from a new hue or uppercase labels. Surfaces are white at low opacity over a near-black page, separated by hairlines. Both themes are first-class: every colour is defined twice and tested in both.\n\nTwo looks are ruled out. The first is **terminal costume**: mono type spread across labels, counts and hosts, tracked uppercase micro-labels, scanlines, grids and glow. The codebase spent a whole pass removing these, and the linter now fails `text-transform: uppercase`. The second is **branded SaaS colour**: a signature hue, gradient CTAs, tinted icon tiles. The only hues are the three status colours and whatever accent the user picks in Settings.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Monochrome by doctrine; colour comes from saved media and status alone.",
      "One sans (Geist) for everything a person reads, one mono (Geist Mono) for text a person might copy or type.",
      "Hairlines, not shadows, separate surfaces. The Ask surface is the documented exception.",
      "Closed token scales for every axis, enforced by `lint:tokens`.",
      "Dark and light themes defined together and tested together.",
      "One user-tintable accent that every filled control must follow."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The No Brand Hue Rule",
        "body": "Hierarchy comes from the ink ladder, never from a new colour. If you are reaching for a hue to make something stand out, use a step up the ladder or the accent fill instead.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Both Themes Rule",
        "body": "Every colour you use must resolve under `:root` and `:root[data-theme=\"light\"]`. A hard-coded white is a light-theme bug; that is how the send button once vanished.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Readable Ink Rule",
        "body": "Anything a person reads is `ink-mute` or stronger, which clears 4.5:1 on the page and in a field in both themes. `ink-faint` is for what nobody has to read.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Follow The Tint Rule",
        "body": "Anything filled with the accent uses `--accent` / `--accent-hover` / `--on-accent`, or it will ignore the user's chosen colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Faces, Two Jobs Rule",
        "body": "Geist for everything a person reads; Geist Mono only for text they might copy or type. Never hand-write a `font-family`. Never set a label, count or host in mono.",
